        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/html": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert\"><h2 id=\"prequel-distributions-over-functions\">Prequel: Distributions over Functions</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">We all know linear regression. Given some data, we assume it has the form <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\epsilon||)</marimo-tex>. We have some data <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(x||)</marimo-tex> and <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(y||)</marimo-tex>, and we want to find the <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(\\hat{\\beta_0}||)</marimo-tex> and <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(\\hat{\\beta_1}||)</marimo-tex> (\"^\" means \"estimated\") that describe a line of best fit. We can do this using OLS (ordinary least squares), and we end up with a linear function <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(f(x) = \\hat{\\beta_0} + \\hat{\\beta_1} x||)</marimo-tex>, that for any <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(x||)</marimo-tex> gives us a prediction for <marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||(y||)</marimo-tex>.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Note ahBut what if we want some notion of the uncertainty of our prediction?</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">For example, look at the two plots below:</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
